WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.101
Funafuti, the capital of Tuvalu: a ring of low islands in the central Pacific,

00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:08.512
home to a nation's government and about half its people.

00:00:09.612 --> 00:00:14.627
Funafuti lies about halfway between Hawaii and Australia, just west of the dateline.

00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:20.377
Seen in three dimensions, Funafuti is the flat top of an enormous extinct volcano.

00:00:20.377 --> 00:00:24.064
The reef ring, about twenty kilometres across, encloses a lagoon.

00:00:24.064 --> 00:00:28.827
Beneath the sea the slopes plunge more than four thousand metres to the ocean floor.

00:00:28.827 --> 00:00:32.910
Note that the lagoon floor in this model is a simplified reconstruction.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.749
Funafuti has a special place in the history of science.

00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:43.526
Between 1896 and 1898, Royal Society expeditions drilled here to test Darwin's theory

00:00:43.526 --> 00:00:49.440
of atolls, reaching about three hundred and forty metres through nothing but reef rock.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.831
The islands themselves are far younger than the reef.

00:00:53.831 --> 00:00:58.488
Reef islands, or motu, are heaps of sand, gravel and rubble made by corals,

00:00:58.488 --> 00:01:02.213
coralline algae, shells and tiny single-celled foraminifera,

00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:04.386
piled up by waves on the reef flat.

00:01:04.386 --> 00:01:07.305
Most formed within the last few thousand years.

00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:11.781
So when we ask whether atolls will drown as the sea rises,

00:01:11.781 --> 00:01:16.961
we are really asking whether waves and reefs can keep supplying and moving that sediment.
